# Saving a response body to disk yourself, via a post-request script and
# the preloaded "ioutil" module (same mechanism 6_shell_auth.http uses for
# "cmd"). See doc/scripting.md's "File reads and running commands" section.
#
# response.body is always the raw response text in httpfly, regardless of
# Content-Type -- it's never pre-parsed into an object for you. That makes
# saving a JSON/text response as simple as writing response.body straight
# to disk; no re-serialization needed. This works for binary content too,
# not just text -- Lua strings are byte arrays, not decoded Unicode text,
# so response.body round-trips through ioutil.write_file byte-for-byte.
# "run -download F" (see 4_download.http) is still the more convenient way
# to save a response as-is: no script needed. Reach for a script instead
# when you need to save something *derived* from the response (a
# re-serialized subset, a different filename per response value, ...)
# rather than the body verbatim.
#
# Run `make httpbin-up` first.

###
# @name SaveJsonResponse
GET http://localhost:8080/get?report=example HTTP/1.1

> {%
  local ioutil = require("ioutil")
  ioutil.write_file("/tmp/httpfly-saved-response.json", response.body)
%}

###
# @name SaveTextResponse
GET http://localhost:8080/robots.txt HTTP/1.1

> {%
  require("ioutil").write_file("/tmp/httpfly-saved-response.txt", response.body)
%}
